.SH NAME
date \- print or set the system date and time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B date
[\fI\,OPTION\/\fR]... [\fB+\fR\fI\,FORMAT\/\fR]
.br
.B date
[\fI\,OPTION\/\fR]... \
\fIMMDDhhmm\/\fR[[\fI\,CC\/\fR]\fI\,YY\/\fR][\fB.\fI\,ss\/\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.\" Add any additional description here
.PP
Display date and time in the given FORMAT.
With \fB\-s\fR, or with MMDDhhmm[[CC]YY][.ss], set the date and time first.
.PP
Mandatory arguments to long options are mandatory for short options too.
.TP
\X'tty: link https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#date-d'\fB\-d, \-\-date=\,STRING\fP\X'tty: link'\/\fR
display time described by STRING, not 'now'
.TP
\X'tty: link https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#date--debug'\fB\-\-debug\fP\X'tty: link'
annotate the parsed date,
and warn about questionable usage to standard error
.TP
\X'tty: link https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#date-f'\fB\-f, \-\-file=\,DATEFILE\fP\X'tty: link'\/\fR
like \fB\-\-date\fR; once for each line of DATEFILE;
if DATEFILE is \-, read names from standard input
.TP
\X'tty: link https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#date-I'\fB\-I[FMT], \-\-iso\-8601[=\,FMT]\fP\X'tty: link'\/\fR
output date/time in ISO 8601 format.
FMT='date' (default), 'hours', 'minutes', 'seconds', or 'ns'
for date and time to the indicated precision.
Example: 2006\-08\-14T02:34:56\-06:00
.TP
\X'tty: link https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#date--resolution'\fB\-\-resolution\fP\X'tty: link'
output the available resolution of timestamps.
Example: 0.000000001
.TP
\X'tty: link https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#date-R'\fB\-R, \-\-rfc\-email\fP\X'tty: link'
output date and time in RFC 5322 format.
Example: Mon, 14 Aug 2006 02:34:56 +0000
.TP
\X'tty: link https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#date--rfc-3339'\fB\-\-rfc\-3339=FMT\fP\X'tty: link'
output date/time in RFC 3339 format.
FMT='date', 'seconds', or 'ns'
for date and time to the indicated precision.
Example: 2006\-08\-14 02:34:56\-06:00
.TP
\X'tty: link https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#date-r'\fB\-r, \-\-reference=\,FILE\fP\X'tty: link'\/\fR
display the last modification time of FILE
.TP
\X'tty: link https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#date-s'\fB\-s, \-\-set=\,STRING\fP\X'tty: link'\/\fR
set time described by STRING
.TP
\X'tty: link https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#date-u'\fB\-u, \-\-utc, \-\-universal\fP\X'tty: link'
print or set Coordinated Universal Time (UTC)
.TP
\X'tty: link https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/date#date--help'\fB\-\-help\fP\X'tty: link'
display this help and exit
.TP
\X'tty: link https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/date#date--version'\fB\-\-version\fP\X'tty: link'
output version information and exit
.PP
All options that specify the date to display are mutually exclusive.
I.e.: \fB\-\-date\fR, \fB\-\-file\fR, \fB\-\-reference\fR, \fB\-\-resolution\fR.
.PP
FORMAT controls the output.  Interpreted sequences are:
.PP
FORMAT  Example    Description
.PP
.PD 0
.TP 6
%%
%           a literal %
.TP 6
%n
\en          a newline
.TP 6
%t
\et          a tab
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.TP 6
%C
20          century; like %Y, except omit last two digits
.TP 6
%y
99          year (last two digits; 00..99)
.TP 6
%Y
1999        year
.TP 6
%g
99          year of ISO week number (last 2 digits; 00\-99); see %G
.TP 6
%G
1999        year of ISO week number; normally useful only with %V
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.TP 6
%a
Sun         locale's abbreviated weekday name
.TP 6
%A
Sunday      locale's full weekday name
.TP 6
%b
Mar         locale's abbreviated month name
.TP 6
%h
Mar         same as %b
.TP 6
%B
March       locale's full month name
.TP 6
%m
12          month (01..12)
.TP 6
%d
31          day of month (01..31)
.TP 7
%e
1          day of month, space padded; same as %_d
.TP 6
%D
12/31/99    date (ambiguous); same as %m/%d/%y
.TP 6
%F
1999\-12\-31  full date; like %+4Y\-%m\-%d
.TP 6
%x
12/31/99    locale's date (can be ambiguous)
.TP 6
%c
\&...         locale's date and time (e.g., Thu Mar  3 23:05 2005)
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.TP 6
%H
23          hour (00..23)
.TP 6
%I
01          hour (01..12)
.TP 7
%k
8          hour, space padded ( 0..23); same as %_H
.TP 7
%l
9          hour, space padded ( 1..12); same as %_I
.TP 6
%M
59          minute (00..59)
.TP 6
%S
60          second (00..60)
.TP 6
%N
123456789   nanoseconds (000000000..999999999)
.TP 6
%s
1970010100  seconds since the Epoch (1970\-01\-01 00:00 UTC)
.TP 6
%p
PM          locale's equivalent of AM or PM; blank if not known
.TP 6
%P
am          like %p, but lower case
.TP 6
%r
1:11:04 PM  locale's 12\-hour clock time
.TP 6
%R
23:59       24\-hour hour and minute; same as %H:%M
.TP 6
%T
23:59:59    time; same as %H:%M:%S
.TP 6
%X
23:59:59    locale's time representation
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.TP 6
%j
365         day of year (001..366)
.TP 6
%q
4           quarter of year (1..4)
.TP 6
%u
7           day of week (1..7); 1 is Monday
.TP 6
%w
6           day of week (0..6); 0 is Sunday
.TP 6
%U
52          week of year; Sunday as first day of week (00..53)
.TP 6
%V
52          ISO week number; Monday as first day of week (01..53)
.TP 6
%W
52          week of year; Monday as first day of week (00..53)
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.TP 7
%z
+0400      +hhmm numeric time zone
.TP 7
%:z
+04:00     +hh:mm numeric time zone
.TP 7
%::z
+04:00:00  +hh:mm:ss numeric time zone
.TP 7
%:::z
+04        numeric time zone to necessary precision; with :
.TP 7
%Z
EDT        alphabetic time zone abbreviation
.PD
.PP
By default, date pads numeric fields with zeroes.
The following optional flags may follow '%':
.PP
.PD 0
.TP 3
\-
(hyphen) do not pad the field
.TP 3
_
(underscore) pad with spaces
.TP 3
0
(zero) pad with zeros
.TP 3
+
pad with zeros, and put '+' before future years with >4 digits
.TP 3
^
use upper case if possible
.TP 3
#
use opposite case if possible
.PD
.PP
After any flags comes an optional field width, as a decimal number;
then an optional modifier, which is either
E to use the locale's alternate representations if available, or
O to use the locale's alternate numeric symbols if available.
.SH EXAMPLES
Convert seconds since the Epoch (1970\-01\-01 UTC) to a date
.IP
\*[mono]$ date --date='@2147483647'\*[/mono]
.PP
Show the time on the west coast of the US (use \fBtzselect\fP(1) to find TZ)
.IP
\*[mono]$ TZ='America/Los_Angeles' date\*[/mono]
.PP
Show the local time for 9AM next Friday on the west coast of the US
.IP
\*[mono]$ date --date='TZ="America/Los_Angeles" 09:00 next Fri'\*[/mono]
.SH "DATE STRING"
.\" NOTE: keep this paragraph in sync with the one in touch.x
The --date=STRING is a mostly free format human readable date string
such as "Sun, 29 Feb 2004 16:21:42 -0800" or "2004-02-29 16:21:42" or
even "next Thursday".  A date string may contain items indicating
calendar date, time of day, time zone, day of week, relative time,
relative date, and numbers.  An empty string indicates the beginning
of the day.  The date string format is more complex than is easily
documented here but is fully described in the info documentation.
